"## Scope\n\nGive `deliver` and `close` a governed way to reach a delivery receipt when a pull request's head advanced after review approval, so a verified deliverable that has already landed on trunk does not become permanently unclosable.\n\n`resolveAcceptedDeliveryHead` pins the accepted SHA to the Test and review receipts. `resolveAcceptedDeliveryAuthority` then selects the pull request whose `headRefOid` equals that accepted SHA. Those two facts are individually correct and jointly brittle: any commit pushed to the branch after approval — including a routine `merge trunk` to pick up a dependency, or a maintainer's suggestion applied in the GitHub UI — moves the pull-request head off the accepted SHA. No pull request then matches, `deliver` refuses `ambiguous-pr`, and `close` refuses `delivery-authority:ambiguous-pr`.\n\nOnce that pull request merges, the condition is terminal: the head cannot be moved back, the current-head path is closed forever, and historical recovery does not apply because it requires a prior accepted-SHA delivery intent that was never recorded (delivery never got far enough to record one).\n\nIn scope: detecting the advanced-head-after-approval condition, distinguishing it from a genuinely unverified head, and providing a governed path to a receipt — for example by recognizing a merged pull request whose head is a descendant of the accepted SHA and whose accepted-SHA content is provably included, or by making the re-verification entry point reachable without misusing the code-rework demote flag.\n\nOut of scope: weakening the exact-head evidence contract, accepting a receipt for content that was never verified, and auto-closing issues without a receipt.\n\n"